# The economics become fascinating

AI causes an **abundance of inference**.

Human expertise supplies **scarcity of accountable judgment**.

So:

[
\text{Value of expert verification}
\propto
\frac{\text{synthetic claims generated}}
{\text{qualified humans able to adjudicate them}}
]

Not literally as a measured law—but directionally that's the economic hypothesis.

For Sanskrit Tantra this asymmetry is insane.

You might eventually have:

```text
10,000,000 AI agents capable of translating Sanskrit

versus

perhaps dozens of living scholars
capable of adjudicating a genuinely difficult
9th-century Śaiva manuscript reading
```

The model does the abundant work.

**The scholar supplies the scarce bit.**

---

# Which means the scholar doesn't lose value to AI

At the top of the epistemic stack, they potentially become **more valuable**.

Before:

```text
Scholar writes translation.
Reader consumes translation.
```

After:

```text
                     ┌ AI translation
                     ├ AI commentary
PRIMARY TEXT ────────┼ AI arguments
                     ├ AI educational media
                     ├ AI research
                     └ AI synthesis
                           │
                           ▼
                NEEDS TRUST ANCHOR
                           │
                           ▼
                       SCHOLAR
```

One expert judgment could propagate into millions of machine interactions.

The important economic question becomes:

> **Who captures the value of that judgment?**

If we don't deliberately build anything, probably the model/platform provider.

Pāṭala can make the alternative:

> **the scholar does.**

---

# And Tantra is almost bizarrely good as the wedge

Compare launching:

> “A universal verification network for all scholarship.”

Impossible cold start.

Versus:

> **“We are establishing the authoritative digital scholarly layer for tantric textual studies.”**

You can actually enumerate:

* scholars;
* texts;
* manuscripts;
* editions;
* translations;
* arguments;
* journals;
* institutions.

And the field has exactly the characteristics that make verification valuable:

**tiny expert pool + difficult languages + manuscript variation + contested dating + inaccessible scholarship + extremely high hallucination risk + rapidly improving AI translation.**

That is an unusually strong test environment.

